{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1156 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f15cefba049b67007c295f8c0673088da4eff88098f486537782cbc5f0a0b0b8" :edb/db-before-t 1155 :edb/db-after-t 1156 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534468 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:34:51.624Z" 13194139534468 true] [17592186082927 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s$, calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t$." 13194139534468 false] [17592186082927 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186082927 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(4t^{2} - 3t^{3})dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}t^{3} - \\frac{3}{4}t^{4} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= \\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3} - \\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2\n$$" 13194139534468 false] [17592186082927 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186082930 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}$." 13194139534468 false] [17592186082930 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186082930 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2e^{-t} + 6t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 2(-e^{-t}) + \\frac{6}{3}t^{3} + C \\\\\n&=-2e^{-t} + 2t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= -2e^{-0} + 2 \\cdot 0^{3} + C \\\\\n- 4 &= -2 + 0 + C \\\\\nC &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = -2 e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time\n\n$$\nt=1\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= -2e^{-1} + 2(1)^{3} - 2 \\\\\n&=-2e^{-1} + 2 - 2 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{e}m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $\\mid v(t) \\mid$, we conclude that the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$" 13194139534468 false] [17592186082930 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289895 13194139534468 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289916 13194139534468 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289920 13194139534468 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289926 13194139534468 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289933 13194139534468 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289902 13194139534468 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289909 13194139534468 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289923 13194139534468 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289940 13194139534468 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289947 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289896 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1089 "q-102693" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1113 #uuid "cbd286de-d18c-5c8c-8baf-fe50f2bdfc52" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289895 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289897 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289898 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289899 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289900 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289901 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1041 "selection" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289899 13194139534468 true] [17592186289896 1110 #uuid "7aabe25f-0df0-5c75-a151-4158c6a44722" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289897 1034 "15\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289897 1111 #uuid "5c38d0bd-2192-5e9b-b150-07a5086649a5" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289898 1034 "9\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289898 1111 #uuid "74e83db5-8638-5454-8e90-b5e4296bc9a6" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289899 1034 "13\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289899 1111 #uuid "56e7dee2-4197-57f1-8ebc-024d6d715bf4" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289900 1034 "17\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289900 1111 #uuid "93740cc5-d830-5826-b8d3-8383a5ef3da6" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289901 1034 "11\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289901 1111 #uuid "b25f5106-8bb7-5827-85fb-54638328c9a4" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289903 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1089 "q-102742" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1113 #uuid "1ac29eea-5742-54dd-bfcd-cbd57bf8e08c" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289902 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{2}{5}(0)^{5/2}+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289904 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289905 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289906 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289907 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289908 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1041 "selection" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289905 13194139534468 true] [17592186289903 1110 #uuid "411ffd2b-061a-59ec-9cf3-f702d8c3d8a4" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289904 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289904 1111 #uuid "68635bc5-8612-5cca-8be1-80f8a4d3de21" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289905 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289905 1111 #uuid "8d189dfd-5fc4-5f1a-85b0-9474fffcd654" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289906 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289906 1111 #uuid "6e747da3-ae63-568a-8c91-04d46faaa91d" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289907 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289907 1111 #uuid "d478c8eb-c35e-515e-b6b9-06132200075e" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289908 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289908 1111 #uuid "08cc742c-dd38-51b2-8359-afdcbed46083" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289910 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1089 "q-19487" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1113 #uuid "1e694ce3-05d1-58d3-bfbc-b58138322825" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289909 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289911 13194139534468 true] [17592186289910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289912 13194139534468 true] [17592186289910 1000 #