he time in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289920 1113 #uuid "e948bb7d-f7d3-5c00-b9a6-b51b039f48d4" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289920 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2({0}^{4})-2({0}^{3})+C \\\\ -4 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}-4.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2({3}^{4})-2({3}^{3})-4 \\\\  & =2(81)-2(27)-4 \\\\  & =162-54-4 \\\\  & =104\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $104\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289922 13194139534468 true] [17592186289921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534468 true] [17592186289921 1041 "field-1" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289922 13194139534468 true] [17592186289921 1110 #uuid "65e9b977-6d14-5d8a-b77c-1273009241ce" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289922 1034 "104" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289922 1111 #uuid "caa4a2ee-dbd2-545c-b653-1e69d669e019" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289924 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1089 "q-351060" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1113 #uuid "2da25941-d469-57cc-a3a6-d483c3ddfc08" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289923 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{4}{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =4+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{4}{1+1}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289925 13194139534468 true] [17592186289924 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534468 true] [17592186289924 1041 "field-1" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289924 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289925 13194139534468 true] [17592186289924 1110 #uuid "53be91c7-9f20-5aab-8680-c267e0647063" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289925 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534468 true] [17592186289925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534468 true] [17592186289925 1111 #uuid "8d5488f4-d374-58ea-8bc4-f38768158635" 13194139534468 tr